00:13:58.728 --> 00:14:01.113
- Someone has to experiment
and go do it first,

00:14:01.113 --> 00:14:05.722
and then, you know, artists
will sit back and watch,

00:14:05.722 --> 00:14:08.335
and be like okay, I like
this, I don't like that.

00:14:08.335 --> 00:14:09.523
You know, I like this part.

00:14:09.523 --> 00:14:11.833
And they'll perfect the
methods, and then, you know,

00:14:11.833 --> 00:14:14.171
and it all pushes the genre forward.

00:14:14.171 --> 00:14:16.568
Anytime that you try to
do something different,

00:14:16.568 --> 00:14:19.342
and you should always try to push forward,

00:14:19.342 --> 00:14:21.770
and whatever you're doin'
it's going to be problems,

00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:22.864
cause it's never been done before,

00:14:22.864 --> 00:14:25.613
so you can't, again, you can't
anticipate all the things

00:14:25.613 --> 00:14:26.763
that's going to happen.

00:14:26.763 --> 00:14:28.005
You know, I don't write anything,

00:14:28.005 --> 00:14:30.062
when I'm recording I don't write lyrics.

00:14:30.062 --> 00:14:33.631
I just listen to the music,
and I formulate the song.

00:14:33.631 --> 00:14:35.672
Like, just off the top of my head.

00:14:35.672 --> 00:14:37.586
I just go over the lyrics again and again

00:14:37.586 --> 00:14:38.487
until I have the song

00:14:38.487 --> 00:14:40.501
and then I just go on in and record it.

00:14:40.501 --> 00:14:43.084
So, it's definitely a God-given talent,

00:14:43.084 --> 00:14:45.550
and it was drive also.

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:46.383
It was drive.

00:14:46.383 --> 00:14:47.360
I mean, it was tough.

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:50.072
I mean, we toured the
whole Eastern Seaboard,

00:14:50.072 --> 00:14:51.846
cause that's where our
records would get played.

00:14:51.846 --> 00:14:55.857
We wasn't national yet, so
we would tour all the way

00:14:55.857 --> 00:14:57.514
up to North Carolina.

00:14:57.514 --> 00:14:58.347
- [Host] Oh, right.

00:14:58.347 --> 00:14:59.180
- We just hit that area.

00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:00.013
Not even Atlanta.

00:15:00.013 --> 00:15:03.707
We just, all the way to
North Carolina we would tour.

00:15:03.707 --> 00:15:07.442
Just jump in the van and
just drive ourselves,

00:15:07.442 --> 00:15:09.554
and just do endless, endless shows.

00:15:09.554 --> 00:15:12.536
I mean with 20 people in
the building sometimes.

00:15:12.536 --> 00:15:13.435
- [Host] Is that right?

00:15:13.435 --> 00:15:14.301
- Yeah.

00:15:14.301 --> 00:15:15.740
- Always believing it would get better

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:16.756
and better and better.

00:15:16.756 --> 00:15:17.873
- Mm Hm.

00:15:17.873 --> 00:15:19.127
I just shot, I shot a commercial,

00:15:19.127 --> 00:15:21.584
when this album had came out.

00:15:21.584 --> 00:15:24.520
And it was like going back in time,

00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:27.697
through all my album
covers, and, you know,

00:15:27.697 --> 00:15:29.320
I had to recreate 'em.

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:32.411
And I realized how easy
it was to recreate.

00:15:32.411 --> 00:15:34.320
I had the same haircut and the same thing

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:36.148
in every single shot.

00:15:36.148 --> 00:15:40.338
So, I don't know if I've been
great at reinventing myself,

00:15:40.338 --> 00:15:44.477
but as far as the company,
we had the opportunity

00:15:44.477 --> 00:15:47.977
to reinvent ourself for, out of necessity.

00:15:49.373 --> 00:15:50.521
With everything that's going on

00:15:50.521 --> 00:15:52.534
you have to reinvent yourself.

00:15:52.534 --> 00:15:54.007
The playing field has changed.

00:15:54.007 --> 00:15:56.620
It's difficult in the music business

00:15:56.620 --> 00:15:59.274
because the internet and
everything that's going on,

00:15:59.274 --> 00:16:01.945
but if you are fearless
it's a beautiful time

00:16:01.945 --> 00:16:03.798
for entrepreneurs.

00:16:03.798 --> 00:16:06.235
The most fun I had in the music business

00:16:06.235 --> 00:16:08.881
was us having this little
rinky dink record company

00:16:08.881 --> 00:16:12.548
and going up radio
stations and other places

00:16:14.235 --> 00:16:17.818
as if we were universal,
and speaking as if

00:16:18.695 --> 00:16:21.751
we sold a hundred million records,

00:16:21.751 --> 00:16:23.748
when in fact we sold nothing.

00:16:23.748 --> 00:16:27.914
So, it feels like that time again for me,

00:16:27.914 --> 00:16:30.972
because the models have to be redefined

00:16:30.972 --> 00:16:33.645
and that's exciting to me.

00:16:33.645 --> 00:16:36.199
I don't know if you're ever done, right?

00:16:36.199 --> 00:16:38.096
It's almost like climbing a mountain,

00:16:38.096 --> 00:16:39.225
and you see another mountain

00:16:39.225 --> 00:16:41.832
and you go to the next
one and the next one.

00:16:41.832 --> 00:16:45.208
My goal was to have one
gold album, and that was it.

00:16:45.208 --> 00:16:47.047
It became more from there,

00:16:47.047 --> 00:16:49.337
and all the way up until The Black Album,

00:16:49.337 --> 00:16:52.144
then it became I want to show

00:16:52.144 --> 00:16:56.075
that artists can ascend
to the executive ranks.

00:16:56.075 --> 00:16:59.475
You know, it just kept redefining itself,

00:16:59.475 --> 00:17:02.901
which is why I took over
the presidency of Def Jam.

00:17:02.901 --> 00:17:05.708
It just keeps reinventing
itself and redefining itself

00:17:05.708 --> 00:17:07.125
every single day.

